From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 08:06:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: skip the modeset for a higher max bpc that can't
 raise the bpc

Raising "max bpc" always caused a modeset, as a higher limit may allow a
higher bpc. But often the limit isn't what holds the bpc down: on a
DisplayPort link without the bandwidth for 10 bpc, NVKMS drives 8 bpc
under a limit of 10 or 12 alike. KWin switches the limit between 10 and
12 with its color accuracy setting, so that blanked the screen for a
modeset that picked 8 bpc again.

NVKMS now records, with the current color, whether the bpc limit lowered
the bpc (nvLimitColorBpcEvo), and reports it through a new read-only
display attribute, NV_KMS_DPY_ATTRIBUTE_CURRENT_COLOR_BPC_LIMITED. If the
limit didn't lower it, the display, the link bandwidth or a requested bpc
did, and a higher limit gives the same bpc. KAPI's getDisplayColorBpc
returns it, and nvidia-drm only does a modeset for a higher limit when it
is set. The current_bpc debugfs file shows it as "Limited".

The flag is conservative: when the limit lowered the bpc and the link
then lowered it further, it stays set, so a higher limit still does a
modeset.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
